        "transcriptHtml": "<p class=\"transcript-note\">Automatic transcript. It may contain small recognition errors; the answer key is authoritative.</p><p><span class=\"ts\">[14:48]</span> Now turn to section three. Track 22, section three. You will hear a tutor and a student discussing a seminar. First, you have some time to look at questions 21 to 26.</p><p><span class=\"ts\">[15:40]</span> Now listen carefully and answer questions 21 to 26. Come in. Hi. Oh, hello, Ahmed. How are you?</p><p><span class=\"ts\">[16:14]</span> Fine, thanks. Have a seat. So, how do you think the seminar went last week? Oh, well, I enjoyed it, yes.</p><p><span class=\"ts\">[16:27]</span> Though I&#x27;m not sure I really followed parts of the discussion that took place. You know about the theory and all that. Well, we can talk about that later, but were you comfortable in a group? Oh, it&#x27;s better, I think, than working on your own,</p><p><span class=\"ts\">[16:45]</span> though you&#x27;re comparing yourself all the time with the other students there. Okay. Well, let&#x27;s talk about how you did and look at some strategies to help you in the future. That would be great. Now, one of the things that students often overlook when they go to seminars</p><p><span class=\"ts\">[17:02]</span> is that you do need to prepare for them. You can&#x27;t rely on other people. I know, and I did look at the results of the experiments we did in class and write them up beforehand, as you said.</p><p><span class=\"ts\">[17:15]</span> Yes, and that was good. It made it easier to analyze them. But you have to do some background reading as well. Did you get the list of articles I sent round?</p><p><span class=\"ts\">[17:26]</span> I started to read them. Okay, well, you&#x27;ll know that for next time. Yes, sure. So, let&#x27;s move on to your participation in the seminar.</p><p><span class=\"ts\">[17:38]</span> Right. Perhaps you can tell me how you think that went. Yeah, well, I&#x27;m not used to talking to more than a couple of people. It&#x27;s very different from the way we learn in my home country.</p><p><span class=\"ts\">[17:51]</span> Yes, I appreciate that. I think I, well, I know I should have included everyone, but I think I kept turning to the person next to me. Is that because you were avoiding eye contact?</p><p><span class=\"ts\">[18:05]</span> I don&#x27;t think so. I&#x27;m not shy. It&#x27;s just habit, I think. Well, that will improve as we do more seminars.</p><p><span class=\"ts\">[18:12]</span> Uh-huh. Another difficulty is knowing when to speak. Like when it&#x27;s your turn? Yes.</p><p><span class=\"ts\">[18:21]</span> I felt I did wait for a pause. Yes, you handled that quite well. The thing I&#x27;m really concerned about is keeping up with the discussion. Does your mind wander off?</p><p><span class=\"ts\">[18:31]</span> Sometimes. I jot down a lot of information, but I still find myself thinking about something else when lots of other students are talking. If there&#x27;s an assignment to do at the end of a group, that usually helps.</p><p><span class=\"ts\">[18:45]</span> I&#x27;m sure it does. Okay. Now, the last thing I want to look at is the role that you play in the seminar. What do you mean?</p><p><span class=\"ts\">[18:54]</span> Well, when students work in groups, they don&#x27;t all behave the same way. Some students are quiet, some look for support, some ask a lot of questions. Oh, that&#x27;s a new idea to me. I don&#x27;t know what I&#x27;m like.</p><p><span class=\"ts\">[19:10]</span> That&#x27;s probably because you&#x27;re thinking about your own performance all the time. I guess so. I mean, should I be different in some way? What I would say is that when we do the next seminar, you should look more at the people</p><p><span class=\"ts\">[19:24]</span> around you. You know, look outside yourself. Like, ask myself how they feel? Yes, or what they&#x27;re looking for from the group.</p><p><span class=\"ts\">[19:34]</span> Okay. It doesn&#x27;t take much, but it&#x27;s important to watch what other students are doing. Okay. I&#x27;ll do that.</p><p><span class=\"ts\">[19:41]</span> Fine. Now, I&#x27;m going to suggest. Before you hear the rest of the conversation, you have some time to look at questions 27 to 30. Now, listen and answer questions 27 to 30.</p><p><span class=\"ts\">[20:33]</span> Now, I&#x27;m going to suggest a couple of strategies for next week&#x27;s seminar. Okay. That&#x27;s great. I need to participate more.</p><p><span class=\"ts\">[20:43]</span> Well, it&#x27;s not a question of saying more, but we need everyone to feel comfortable about giving their views. Then the discussion is better. Yes.</p><p><span class=\"ts\">[20:53]</span> So, you&#x27;re a confident person. Should I make sure I&#x27;m near someone who&#x27;s quiet? You can do, but it&#x27;s more about how well you pay attention to other students. Okay.</p><p><span class=\"ts\">[21:06]</span> So I need to be attentive. Yes. And then encourage someone else to say more by saying, what did you mean when you said, or what do you think about the idea that-</p><p><span class=\"ts\">[21:18]</span> That way I&#x27;m talking. Yes. But you&#x27;ll find that other people will talk too. You&#x27;ll all start to get really involved.</p><p><span class=\"ts\">[21:28]</span> Right. They&#x27;re good suggestions. The other thing that can really help is the way you take notes. I know I write down everything, but I should be stricter with myself.</p><p><span class=\"ts\">[21:39]</span> Well, you actually need to think a few days ahead. Really? Yes. What&#x27;s the topic, and what&#x27;s the best way of making notes?</p><p><span class=\"ts\">[21:49]</span> I see. So, I have a strategy when I walk in the room. Exactly. Then when you read through them later, they&#x27;ll make sense, and you won&#x27;t have to write them out again.</p><p><span class=\"ts\">[22:00]</span> I always have to do that. The other thing, I would say, is that you should include a small column in your notes, where you can jot down things you want to go back to before the seminar ends. Like a reminder.</p><p><span class=\"ts\">[22:13]</span> Yes. Notes aren&#x27;t just for later. You can use them as a prompt when there&#x27;s a pause in the discussion. That&#x27;s been really helpful.</p><p><span class=\"ts\">[22:22]</span> Okay. See you in class tomorrow. Thanks. That is the end of section three.</p><p><span class=\"ts\">[22:31]</span> You now have half a minute to check your answers.</p>"
